// StdlibWhitelist — the explicit list of stdlib modules shv2 loads into EVERY user
// program, plus the machinery that keeps an UNUSED whitelisted module byte-for-byte
// invisible.
//
// ⭐ WHY A WHITELIST, NOT v1's ALL-OR-NOTHING PREPEND. v1 (`StdlibLoader.PrependStdlib`)
// and the bootstrap (`BuildCache.WithStdlibSources`) load ALL of `stdlib/`. shv2 does not:
// stdlib support grows one module at a time, each module gated on the language features it
// needs (Map/FilePath/String-methods each have their own blockers, see PLAN.md). This file
// is the ONE place that set is stated — `whitelistedStdlibRelativePaths()`. Grow that list
// as modules become compilable; adding an entry is the whole cost of turning a module on.
//
// ⭐ HOW A LISTED MODULE REACHES USER CODE. Each whitelisted file is registered into the
// query database (`fileChanged`) exactly like a user source, so it flows through the SAME
// tokenize → signature-index → parse → merge spine with no query-spine change. Its
// declarations therefore populate `funcSignatures`, and a user program can call `Clock.nowMs()`
// though the program itself contains no Clock. Registration ORDER does not matter here the way
// it does in the bootstrap's `PrependStdlib`: shv2's `queryProgramSignatures` sweeps EVERY
// file's tokens for declarations before any file is parsed, so visibility is order-independent
// (the merge order only decides function order in the module, and DFE prunes the unused ones).
//
// ⭐ THE ONE INVARIANT: an UNUSED whitelisted module changes NOTHING. A program that never
// calls Clock must compile to the exact bytes it did before Clock was whitelisted — same
// x64 goldens, same wasm/arm64 output. Two mechanisms compose to guarantee that, and the
// SECOND is the subtle one this file exists for:
//
//   1. Dead-function elimination (`DeadFunctionElimination.eliminateDeadFunctions`) drops
//      every whitelisted function no reachable code calls, at the target-neutral backend
//      entry — so an unused Clock never reaches isel/regalloc/encode and never lands in a
//      golden, on any target.
//
//   2. ⚠ But DFE runs at the Std tier, AFTER `scanRuntimeUsage` has already decided which
//      RUNTIME FLOOR the program carries (heap / GT scheduler / wall clock) by walking the
//      MERGED MAXON MODULE. Clock.nowMs's body calls `__gt_now_ns` and WallClock.nowUnixSeconds's
//      calls `__clock_now_unix_s`, so a NAIVE load would make `scanRuntimeUsage` install the GT
//      scheduler + the wall-clock `.data` slots for a program that never reads a clock — slots
//      DFE does not prune (it prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.data`). MEASURED before this gate: a bare
//      `return 7` grew from 1512 to 2488 code bytes and gained 24 `.data` slots.
//
//      So the whitelisted functions no user code reaches must not feed the runtime-floor
//      decision either. `stdlibWhitelistSkipSet` computes exactly that set — whitelisted AND
//      unreachable from `main` — and `scanRuntimeUsage` skips it. ⭐ USER functions are NEVER
//      skipped (their present-code floor semantics are unchanged), so the existing corpus is
//      untouched; only the INJECTED whitelist code is reachability-gated. This is sound for
//      Clock because a Clock function is reached ONLY by direct `call`/`funcAddr` — never by a
//      witness dispatch or an `.rdata` reloc — so call-graph reachability from `main` is EXACT
//      for it (a Maxon-tier reachability that missed a witness edge would be unsound in general,
//      which is why this skip is scoped to the whitelist rather than applied to all functions).
//
// ⭐ THE COLLISION RULE. A whitelisted module must declare no name a user program declares and
// no builtin type/name.
//   • FUNCTION-name collisions — a user `Clock.nowMs` against the whitelisted one, or two
//     whitelisted modules — are caught LOUDLY by the whole-program duplicate-function check
//     (E3006, `commitFuncSignatures` in ParseStaging), because a whitelisted file merges through
//     the identical path a user file does. Nothing to add here; `stdlib-whitelist.md` pins it.
//   • TYPE-name-vs-builtin collisions are the maintainer's responsibility. shv2 has no
//     builtin-type-redeclaration diagnostic at all — a user `type String` compiles today as a
//     distinct nominal — so enforcing one is a general language matter, not a whitelist one.
//     Clock declares `Clock`/`WallClock` and time typealiases, none of them builtin, so it
//     cannot hit this. Do not whitelist a module that redeclares a builtin.
//   • ⚠ FUNCTION-name-vs-BARE-BUILTIN collisions are SILENT — and this is why `stdlib/Sleep.maxon`
//     is NOT on the list above, though the compiler can now compile it. `Parser.parseCallNamed`
//     recognizes a handful of BARE names (`print`, `sleep`, `trunc`, `runProcess`, the math
//     intrinsics) BEFORE `emitCall` consults any registry, so a CALL to such a name never reaches
//     a declaration of it. Whitelisting Sleep would therefore load a module no ordinary call site
//     can reach — `sleep(5)` still emits the builtin's `__gt_sleep` — while charging the
//     whitelist's per-compile cost (MEASURED at +677 allocations and ~30 KB on EVERY compile,
//     flat across a 32x scale ladder) for zero delivered capability. Worse, the name would have
//     TWO routes: `let f = sleep` is not a call site, so it takes the address of the whitelisted
//     `stdlib.sleep`, whose body reaches the same entry the long way round.
//     ⚠ The shadowing is NOT the whitelist's doing and predates it: a user file declaring its own
//     `function sleep` already compiles with the declaration silently unlinked, no diagnostic
//     (measured on plain user code). The repair is to delete the bare-name builtin and let the
//     whitelisted declaration be authoritative — its own rung, because it moves every committed
//     golden that calls `sleep` (COUNTED: 15 goldens across 7 specs — async-sleep, async-promise-drop,
//     async-stack-growth, async-subprocess, builtins-clock, spawn-read-line, streaming-subprocess),
//     changes `async-sleep.float-arg-rejected`'s pinned stderr, and must first answer the
//     diagnostic-attribution gap below.
//     ⇒ THE RULE: do not whitelist a module whose function name a bare builtin already claims.
//     Retire the builtin first.
//
// ⭐ THE OPEN MECHANISM GAP: A DIAGNOSTIC THAT ORIGINATES INSIDE WHITELISTED STDLIB SOURCE.
// A whitelisted module is a real compilation unit, so a rejection raised in ITS body is positioned
// at ITS path — an absolute `…/stdlib/Foo.maxon:L:C` naming a file the user never opened, for a
// mistake they made at a call site somewhere else. Nothing renders it in terms of the caller, and
// no spec can pin it (the runner rewrites only the fragment's own path).
//
// ⚠ THIS IS GENERAL TO THE WHITELIST, NOT A QUIRK OF ANY ONE MODULE. It fires wherever a
// whitelisted body bottoms out in something target-gated or otherwise refusable, which is exactly
// what a stdlib leaf is FOR — every module in the queue behind Clock ends in a `__Builtins.*`
// intrinsic. Clock is only spared today because its callers are `Clock.nowMs()`-shaped, so
// `SemanticCheck`'s E3104 span already lands inside `stdlib/Clock.maxon` on a non-x64 target and
// no test compiles a Clock program for one.
//
// ⚠ WHICH ROUTE MAKES IT USER-VISIBLE WAS MEASURED, because guessing it wrong sends the next rung
// after the wrong repro. With the entry temporarily added, a plain `sleep(1)` compiled for wasm is
// STILL anchored at the USER's span — the bare builtin claims the call site, so the whitelisted body
// is never entered. What reaches the gap is the SECOND route named above: `let f = sleep` takes the
// whitelisted declaration's address, and `f(5)` for wasm reports E3104 at `stdlib/Sleep.maxon:6:2`,
// a file the user never opened. Once the bare-name builtin is RETIRED, the plain call takes that
// route too and the gap becomes the common case rather than the exotic one.
//
// Whoever retires the bare-name builtins owns this: a diagnostic raised in whitelisted source needs a
// caller-side anchor (or an explicit "…, required by <call site>" note) before the whitelist grows a
// module users reach through a target-gated leaf.

// The directory name walked for, and a generous bound on how far up the tree to look. A
// checkout is shallow (`<root>/maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2.exe` is three levels under the
// `stdlib/` that sits at `<root>`), so 64 is far more than enough while still terminating on a
// pathological path rather than looping forever.
let StdlibDirName = "stdlib"
let MaxStdlibSearchDepth = 64

// Name → merged-module function index, for the reachability walk below — the Maxon-tier twin of
// `DeadFunctionElimination.FuncIndexByName`.
typealias WhitelistFuncIndexByName = Map with (ByteArray, FuncIndex)

// The set of function names a scan (`scanRuntimeUsage`) must SKIP — whitelisted functions the
// program does not reach. A `Set with ByteArray` for O(1) membership, exactly like `DestructorSet`.
export typealias FunctionNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// The whitelisted-module paths a `Project` records. A DISTINCTLY-named alias rather than
// QueryDatabase's `FilePathArray`, whose name collides with a member typealias inside stdlib's
// `type Directory` and so does not resolve from every compiler file (`DiagnosticArray`, the same
// `Array with T` shape under a unique name, resolves cross-file fine — the name is the difference).
export typealias WhitelistPathArray = Array with FilePath

// ⭐ THE WHITELIST. The stdlib modules loaded into every compile, as paths RELATIVE to the
// checkout root (the parent of `stdlib/`). This is the ONE place the set is stated — grow it as
// modules become whitelistable, each new entry obeying the collision rule in this file's header.
function whitelistedStdlibRelativePaths() returns StringArray
	var paths = StringArray.create()
	paths.push("stdlib/Clock.maxon")
	return paths
end 'whitelistedStdlibRelativePaths'

// Locate the checkout's `stdlib/` by walking UP from the COMPILER's own executable directory.
//
// ⚠ NOT from `Directory.currentPath()` (v1's `findStdlibPath`): the spec runner and `run_program`
// compile a program in a throwaway temp dir, so the working directory has no `stdlib/` above it.
// The compiler binary always lives inside the checkout, so its own path is the reliable anchor —
// the same reason the bootstrap's `FindStdlibPath` (0-Compiler.cs) walks up from
// `AppContext.BaseDirectory`. A failure to locate it is a hard, LOUD error rather than a silent
// skip: every compile now depends on the whitelist, so a missing stdlib is a real environment
// problem the user must see, not a Clock call that mysteriously reports E3004.
function locateStdlibDir() returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let exePath = try Process.executablePath() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	var dir = try exePath.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound

	var depth = 0
	while depth < MaxStdlibSearchDepth 'search'
		// Interpolated so the argument is a committed `String`: a bare module-`let` literal stays
		// untyped and matches BOTH `FilePath.join` overloads (String / FilePath) — an E3007 ambiguity —
		// whereas a String VALUE selects `join(String)` cleanly (the same reason `root.join(entry)` below,
		// with `entry` a `StringArray` element, needs no such coaxing).
		let candidate = dir.join("{StdlibDirName}")
		if Directory.exists(candidate) 'found'
			return candidate
		end 'found'
		// `parent()` throws at the filesystem root — the correct place for the search to give up.
		dir = try dir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
		depth = depth + 1
	end 'search'

	throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
end 'locateStdlibDir'

// Register every whitelisted stdlib module into the project's query database, so its declarations
// are visible to user code. Called by `loadProject` AFTER the user's own sources are loaded (so a
// directory build's output name still derives from a user file, not from Clock, and an empty user
// project still fails with `noSourceFiles` rather than silently compiling only the whitelist).
//
// ⚠ LOUD, NEVER SILENT. A whitelisted path that does not exist under `stdlib/` is a hard error
// naming it (`whitelistModuleMissing`); an unreadable one propagates `readError` naming it; a
// PARSE failure surfaces through the ordinary parse-diagnostic path (`queryParseOps` →
// `reportParseError`), which already renders the file path. There is no arm that drops a
// whitelisted module quietly.
export function loadStdlibWhitelist(project Project) throws CompileError
	let entries = whitelistedStdlibRelativePaths()
	if entries.count() == 0 'empty'
		return
	end 'empty'

	let stdlibDir = try locateStdlibDir()
	let root = try stdlibDir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound

	for entry in entries 'each'
		let resolved = root.join(entry)
		if not File.exists(resolved) 'missing'
			throw CompileError.whitelistModuleMissing(resolved)
		end 'missing'
		try loadOneSourceFile(project, path: resolved)
		project.stdlibWhitelistPaths.push(resolved)
	end 'each'
end 'loadStdlibWhitelist'

// The function names each whitelisted file contributed, read back from its parse artifact (a memo
// HIT — `queryAllModule` already parsed it). A `ByteArrayArray` rather than a set because the
// names are unique by construction (E3006 rejects a duplicate) and the sole consumer iterates them.
function whitelistedFunctionNames(project Project) returns ByteArrayArray throws CompileError
	var names = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for path in project.stdlibWhitelistPaths 'eachFile'
		let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)
		for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
			names.push(func.name)
		end 'eachFunc'
	end 'eachFile'
	return names
end 'whitelistedFunctionNames'

// The reachable-from-`main` closure over the merged Maxon module, as a set of function NAMES.
// The Maxon-tier twin of `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf`: the FOUR ops that name a callee
// are edges — `call`, `tryCall`, `functionRef` (a TAKEN ADDRESS is a static edge even though the
// call consuming it is indirect) and `asyncCall`. Every other variant names no static callee.
//
// A callee that is not a module function — an as-yet-uninstalled `__gt_*`/`__clock_*` runtime
// helper, which is added only during/after lowering — is simply not queued, exactly as
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction` handles the same case.
function reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module MaxonModule) returns FunctionNameSet
	var byName = WhitelistFuncIndexByName.create()
	for i in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'index'
		byName.upsert(module.getFunc(i).name, value: i)
	end 'index'

	var reached = BoolArray.create()
	reached.resize(module.funcCount())
	var worklist = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var names = FunctionNameSet.create()

	markReachable(UserEntryName.toByteArray(), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		markMaxonCallees(module, func: module.getFunc(idx), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'walk'

	return names
end 'reachableMaxonFunctionNames'

// Mark `name` reachable and queue its body. A name no module function carries (a runtime helper,
// or a root whose runtime this program never installs) is not found and not queued — nothing to
// walk. Mirrors `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction`.
function markReachable(name ByteArray, byName WhitelistFuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	let already = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")
	if already 'alreadyReached'
		return
	end 'alreadyReached'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	names.insert(name)
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markReachable'

// Walk `block.opRefs` DIRECTLY — not `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which allocates a fresh op-index array
// per function. This runs at the Maxon tier BEFORE any block-rebuilding pass, so `opRefs` holds no
// orphan ops (the concern that forces `liveOpIndices` at the Std tier), which is exactly why
// `scanRuntimeUsage` walks the same way here. A terminator names no callee, so visiting only the body
// ops in `opRefs` is complete for reachability.
function markMaxonCallees(module MaxonModule, func IrFunction, byName WhitelistFuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			markMaxonCalleeEdge(op, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'markMaxonCallees'

// The static reachability edge one `MaxonOp` names, if any. The arms RANGE over the whole union in
// declaration order, ending at the current last variant (`witnessDispatch`), so a variant appended
// past it FAILS TO COMPILE here rather than being silently swallowed — the band-append invariant
// working in the reviewer's favour (MaxonDialect's header), exactly as `markCalleeOf` relies on it.
// A new variant that carries a callee name must be added as its own arm.
function markMaxonCalleeEdge(op MaxonOp, byName WhitelistFuncIndexByName, reached BoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _) then markReachable(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		// `literal` … `stringLiteral`: literals, arithmetic, comparisons, branches, returns, and the
		// three `var`-access ops — none names a Maxon function.
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _) then markReachable(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		// `throwError` … `panicAbort`: the error terminators name no callee.
		throwError to panicAbort then break 'op'
		functionRef(_, funcName) then markReachable(funcName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		// `indirectCall` calls through a code POINTER a `functionRef` above already credited.
		indirectCall then break 'op'
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _) then markReachable(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		// `await` … `witnessDispatch`: an await consumes a promise VALUE, and a witness dispatch reads
		// an `.rdata` table — neither names a static Maxon callee here.
		await to witnessDispatch then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'markMaxonCalleeEdge'

// ⭐ The set every pre-elimination pass must SKIP: whitelisted functions the program does not reach
// (`scanRuntimeUsage`'s floor scan, `checkCalls`'s E3104 target gate). This is the whole of the
// "unused whitelist is invisible" guarantee. Empty when there is no whitelist, and — for a program
// that DOES reach a whitelisted function — contains only the sibling whitelisted functions that
// program left untouched (which contribute no floor, so skipping them is neutral): the floor comes
// from the reachable ones, scanned as normal.
export function stdlibWhitelistSkipSet(project Project, module MaxonModule) returns FunctionNameSet throws CompileError
	var skip = FunctionNameSet.create()
	if project.stdlibWhitelistPaths.count() == 0 'noWhitelist'
		return skip
	end 'noWhitelist'

	let whitelistedNames = try whitelistedFunctionNames(project)
	var whitelistedSet = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for name in whitelistedNames 'seed'
		whitelistedSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	// ⭐ COMMON-CASE SHORT-CIRCUIT — the whole non-Clock corpus takes it. If no op ANYWHERE names a
	// whitelisted callee, no whitelisted function can be reached, so ALL of them are skipped — reached
	// by ONE allocation-free O(ops) scan, WITHOUT the O(functions) name->index map and reached-array
	// the from-`main` walk allocates. That map is the whole per-compile memory cost of the whitelist
	// on a program that never touches it, so skipping it keeps the cost a per-compile CONSTANT (Clock's
	// fixed parse) instead of growing with the user program. It stays EXACT: a whitelisted function
	// referenced by nothing is reachable from nothing.
	if not moduleReferencesWhitelisted(module, whitelisted: whitelistedSet) 'noneReferenced'
		for name in whitelistedNames 'skipAll'
			skip.insert(name)
		end 'skipAll'
		return skip
	end 'noneReferenced'

	// A whitelisted function IS referenced somewhere — resolve exactly which the program REACHES from
	// `main` and skip only the untouched siblings. This precise walk (and its maps) runs ONLY for a
	// program that actually uses the whitelist, which is small and rare.
	let reachable = reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module)
	for name in whitelistedNames 'classify'
		if not reachable.contains(name) 'unreachable'
			skip.insert(name)
		end 'unreachable'
	end 'classify'

	return skip
end 'stdlibWhitelistSkipSet'

// True iff any live op in the module names a WHITELISTED callee — the allocation-free pre-scan that
// lets a program which never references the whitelist skip the reachability walk entirely. It reads
// the same four callee-naming variants `markMaxonCalleeEdge` does, and is a SEPARATE band match on
// purpose: it runs on every compile, so it must allocate nothing (no boxed callee union, no map/queue)
// — it only asks "is one present?". Both matches are guarded by the same band-append invariant.
function moduleReferencesWhitelisted(module MaxonModule, whitelisted FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐ SKIP the whitelisted functions' OWN bodies. The whitelisted set is internally connected
		// (`Clock.elapsedMs` calls `Clock.nowMs`), so scanning their bodies would find an intra-whitelist
		// edge on EVERY compile and defeat this short-circuit. The exact question is "does the USER
		// program cross INTO the whitelist?", and the first edge into the whitelisted set on any path
		// from `main` is made by a NON-whitelisted caller — so scanning only non-whitelisted bodies is
		// the exact condition. (A whitelisted-only reference is unreachable-from-`main` anyway, so a
		// false negative here is impossible; a false positive would merely trigger the exact full walk.)
		if whitelisted.contains(func.name) 'skipWhitelistedBody'
			continue
		end 'skipWhitelistedBody'

		// Direct `block.opRefs` walk — allocation-free, and safe for the same reason `markMaxonCallees`
		// and `scanRuntimeUsage` use it at this tier (pre-pipeline: no orphan ops; callees are body ops).
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("moduleReferencesWhitelisted: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("moduleReferencesWhitelisted: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				if opNamesWhitelistedCallee(op, whitelisted: whitelisted) 'found'
					return true
				end 'found'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return false
end 'moduleReferencesWhitelisted'

// Whether one `MaxonOp` names a callee in `whitelisted`. Same band structure — and same band-append
// protection — as `markMaxonCalleeEdge` (a variant appended past `witnessDispatch` fails to compile
// here rather than being silently swallowed).
function opNamesWhitelistedCallee(op MaxonOp, whitelisted FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	return match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _) gives whitelisted.contains(callee)
		literal to stringLiteral gives false
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _) gives whitelisted.contains(callee)
		throwError to panicAbort gives false
		functionRef(_, funcName) gives whitelisted.contains(funcName)
		indirectCall gives false
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _) gives whitelisted.contains(callee)
		await to witnessDispatch gives false
	end 'op'
end 'opNamesWhitelistedCallee'
